      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: clamp Cursor plan usage percent to 100%",
        "body": "## Summary\n\nOn a usage-based Cursor plan in overage (`used > limit`), the headline plan-usage percent could exceed 100%. In `CursorStatusProbe`, the plan-ratio branch computed `(planUsedRaw / planLimitRaw) * 100` **without** the `normalizeTotalPercent` clamp that every sibling branch in the same function applies — so e.g. 15000/10000 flowed through as `150`, into `RateWindow.usedPercent`.\n\nOverage is already surfaced separately (on-demand USD), so the headline percent is meant to cap at 100.\n\n## Fix\n\nWrap that one branch in `normalizeTotalPercent` (`max(0, min(100, v))`), identical to the other branches:\n\n```diff\n-            (planUsedRaw / planLimitRaw) * 100\n+            normalizeTotalPercent((planUsedRaw / planLimitRaw) * 100)\n```\n\nOne line. No behavior change for in-range plans.\n\n## Test\n\nAdded `plan ratio caps at 100 percent when usage exceeds the limit` — `used: 15000, limit: 10000` now yields `planPercentUsed == 100` (was `150`). Red before the source change, green after.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Source compiles clean on Linux (`swift:6.3.3`, `swift build --product CodexBarCLI` — `CursorStatusProbe.swift` is in `CodexBarCore`).\n- The new test lives in the macOS `CodexBarTests` target, so the `swift-test-macos` CI job validates it.\n\nSmall and self-contained — happy to adjust or close if the clamp-on-overage behavior isn't wanted.\n",
        "branch": "fix-cursor-plan-percent-clamp",
